      "content": [
        {
          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"lawrence-women-in-love-bdddeb99cc17cb63\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.94,\"analysis\":\"The embedded formula “I love you” is construed as an avowal that presents a loving relation as true. Its occurrence is hypothetical and negated—“Nor can I say”—because the speaker’s experience allegedly transcends the separate subjects presupposed by the formula. That philosophical rejection does not alter the formula’s predominantly truth-conditional force. There is no independent undertaking force or evidence that the words function as reflex-like affective discharge.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The phrase is simultaneously quoted and hypothetical, but the governing negated modal makes “hypothetical” the best status. The surrounding marriage context could weakly suggest commitment, yet this particular formula is treated as a statement inadequate to the experience, not as an undertaking.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"hypothetical\",\"description\":\"A quoted possible utterance embedded under the negated claim “Nor can I say”; it is invoked as a formula that cannot adequately be uttered in the imagined state of transcendent oneness.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"The passage contrasts ordinary verbal avowal with a mystical conception of union in which the separate “I” and “you” have disappeared. “I love you” is therefore presented as propositionally sincere but conceptually inadequate—speech belonging to separable persons—rather than as false, performative, or reflexive. The claim that their experience is “beyond love” concerns the limits of the formula and the surrounding metaphysics, not an unrepresented force within the formula itself.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Nor can I say ‘I love you,’ when I have ceased to be, and you have ceased to be”\",\"supports\":\"The phrase is a hypothetical, explicitly unavailable utterance whose ordinary form presupposes distinct first- and second-person subjects.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Speech travels between the separate parts. But in the perfect One there is perfect silence of bliss.”\",\"supports\":\"The barrier to utterance is the passage’s metaphysics of dissolved separateness, not expressive discharge or a relational undertaking.\",\"confidence\":0.97},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Immediately beforehand, the narration says that even the sincerely whispered “I love you, I love you” was “not the real truth” because the experience was “something beyond love.”\",\"supports\":\"The formula is understood as a sincere truth-presenting avowal that fails to capture the full experience; mode and adequacy are distinguished.\",\"confidence\":0.96},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The union is described as “a new one, a new, paradisal unit regained from the duality.”\",\"supports\":\"Explains why the ordinary subject–object proposition is rejected while supplying no independent basis for P, E, or O.\",\"confidence\":0.95}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the conventional force of the invoked formula. Its hypothetical negation and philosophical inadequacy are fully representable through utterance status and context; they do not create residual core-force pressure requiring O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
